e who  had come from all kinds of villages  where we had been we're just going to  enter the camp now the IDP in dunya  I can guarantee within about five  minutes we're gonna get mobbed it's kids  enough families they said there was  nearly ten thousand people and this was  just one of the camps which had sprung  up out of nowhere we all knew there were  a whole load more people out there who  haven't even got to the camps so these  camps were just a small portion of what  was happening around the country  particularly in that province  they'd have to flee their villages  they're living in abject poverty and  they're sleeping and surviving some  pretty tough conditions they still find  in them to be like children to play  [Music]  [Applause]  we were told that there were many people  who are attacked so we went to one tent  and they said they had people who were  victims of a machete attack little girl  how old this little toddler who's  stumbling along you know it was only two  years old and she's got this huge big  slash across her face I mean it's it's  really difficult to imagine anyone who  could do that  I mean you've taken a machete and you  smashed it onto a little child's face  but then he tried again because she had  a big gash on the back of her head the  the cruelty is pretty unimaginable and  then her father came interview his face  was just a picture of misery and shock  he'd seen his wife already killed and  then they started attacking his children  and so he intervened and he was covered  in about scars it was just quite a shock  to see the kind of violence that can be  inflicted and then we discovered that  she had an older sister he'd also been  machete  they'd chopped her hand off and we  interviewed this family who were  grateful just to be alive yet were  carrying the scars of the most  horrendous violence and that was that  was quite a shocking scene to come  across and to film this that's to watch  me like someone who wants to kill a  goats  we're hearing that little girl would  count the story of her attack was really  quite chilling this was just this young  little girl talking like that I think  there are so many problems in the world  that it perhaps just feels like one more  but what I don't think the world  realizes is just how much suffering  there is in the DRC the images of the  children will not go away when there is  such evidence of such large-scale  suffering and the government is  continuing to say there isn't a  humanitarian crisis  it's quite hard to remain unemotional  about that
 

